{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question","id":953636,"title":"Animal Kinfolk question","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-08-18T20:52:42-07:00","id":953636,"title":"Animal Kinfolk question","content":"I'm just wondering how you guys handle animal kinfolk.<br /> <br /> Are all species that are viable mates considered kinfolk?<br /> Like, do you run it as all wolves are considered Garou kinfolk? Or is it just certain ones with Garou lineage.<br /> Same for the other Fera.<br /> Rokea can mate with any species of shark. Do you consider all sharks then viable as kinfolk for Rokea mating.<br /> In humans, the kinfolk gene dies out after a few generations. Do you have the same thing happen with the animal side of it? And if so, how many generations does being kinfolk last. I know Pure Breed can play into it for Garou, but plenty of other Fera don't have Pure Breed as a background.<br /> <br /> And I'm curious for both canon answers and just how people run it at their tables.<br /> <br /> Personally, I had always assumed that any member of that animal species would be considered kinfolk and be viable for honorable mating.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-18T21:53:31-07:00","id":953668,"title":"","content":"IIRC, non-human animals are no exception to the issues of being kinfolk vs. being normal animals.<br /> <br /> In any population one of the Shifter groups is actively managing, I'd assume most of the animals are kin though, because of the much faster breeding rates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-18T22:23:46-07:00","id":953674,"title":"","content":"Only those animals descending from a changer are kin, as far as I know. We have no reason to believe that animal kinfolk lose their kin status any faster or slower than human kinfolk.<br /> <br /> More importantly, mates don't need to be kin to produce kin or even exceptional garou, so it follows that just being a wolf - just like being a human - does not necessarily convey kin status. The mere possibility of mating with non-kin indicates that not all members of a species are kin, barring extreme outliers. Offspring of such matings have a 50% chance to produce kin based on page 203 of the Player's Guide to the Garou.<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> The tendency of Werewolf's writers to refer generally to all wolf or other non-homid populations for a given Breed as 'kin' is a complicating factor, but I don't think it's meant to be taken literally so much as a figurative 'they are all <i>possible</i> kin or the potential parents of kin'. E.g., the loss of the Thylacine wiped out the Bunyip's lupine kin, but whether or not any of the remaining Thylacines by the time they went extinct were <i>actual</i> kin is irrelevant - they were all either kin or could give birth to/sire a future kin population.<br /> <br /> We see the same language used to refer to ethnic groups that various Garou tribes and Fera breeds habitually mate with, whether or not those individuals are actually kin or not. If memory serves, they usually don't capitalize the term for population groups that are considered 'kin' in this communal-potential perspective but they do capitalize Kin for actual Kinfolk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5307,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5307-loomer","user":"loomer","date":". 08-19-2016, 01:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5307-loomer","name":"loomer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5307_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T06:41:40-07:00","id":953800,"title":"","content":"Given the low populations of some animals, like tigers, I would personally rule that the majority of the survivors in the wild are animal kinfolk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T07:37:42-07:00","id":953831,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953800#post953800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given the low populations of some animals, like tigers, I would personally rule that the majority of the survivors in the wild are animal kinfolk.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, the kin tend to be secret, unmonitored populations, by and large. Again, the WoD has a kind of bizarre gothic atmosphere fostered by having everyone cluster closer to large cities and leave the wilderness alone, so there's more of it and, thus, more wildlife, with numbers unknown to most surveys.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T07:38:17-07:00","id":953832,"title":"","content":"So follow up thoughts.<br /> If only a portion of the animals are kinfolk, that would go a long way towards explaining the low Gurahl numbers, since most bears nowadays wouldn't be kinfolk (which is a sad thought imo). That also means that Rokea mating once or twice a century puts their number of kinfolk extremely low, even with being able to mate with any shark.<br /> <br /> Because this came up in another thread. What percentage of the rat population do you think are Ratkin kinfolk? Using NYC as an example again. There are over 2 million rats in the city. I'm curious how many of them are kinfolk then.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T08:22:30-07:00","id":953850,"title":"","content":"To add to the not all animals are kin...also keep in mind CB20 acknowledges that while there are wild pigs in Europe and Asia, none of them left are from any kin lineages. So even if you could pull a Grondr out of time or something along those times, it wouldn't be able to reproduce. <br /> <br /> <br /> As for the ratkin, god....I have never even bothered to think of how many rats would be kin, just because of how much rats can reproduce to begin with...the answer in my head was always &quot;enough to not worry&quot;...<br /> <br /> Now here's a question...rokea can breed with any shark....but does that mean a great white rokea and swim up to a mako kin? or should it stick along certain species lines? Same question goes for breeds like the Nagah (king cobra and rattler?)...since some of these breeds don't cover things such as the Mokole gift &quot;Steal Shape&quot; or whatever mokole rites helped create the Bunyip and Kucha Ekundu (assuming that wasn't the gift Steal Shape to begin with).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T08:26:31-07:00","id":953854,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953850#post953850\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To add to the not all animals are kin...also keep in mind CB20 acknowledges that while there are wild pigs in Europe and Asia, none of them left are from any kin lineages. So even if you could pull a Grondr out of time or something along those times, it wouldn't be able to reproduce.</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be able to reproduce fine; The mate does not *need* to be kin for reproduction purposes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T08:28:51-07:00","id":953857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953854#post953854\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It would be able to reproduce fine; The mate does not *need* to be kin for reproduction purposes.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> What I meant was bring another Grondr into the world, not just bringing a new (non kin) pig into the world","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T08:39:45-07:00","id":953864,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953850#post953850\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the ratkin, god....I have never even bothered to think of how many rats would be kin, just because of how much rats can reproduce to begin with...the answer in my head was always &quot;enough to not worry&quot;...</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, given that Rite of the Birthing Plague doesn't cost anything to use, I was thinking of an Apocalypse scenario where they infect 100% of their kin and the aftereffects of that. So I was trying to come up with a ballpark percentage of rats that were kinfolk.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953857#post953857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I meant was bring another Grondr into the world, not just bringing a new (non kin) pig into the world</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see why they couldn't mate and produce kin, and then mate with the kin (incest-y I know, but it <i>does</i> happen in nature) to produce new Grondr eventually. It would take a couple of generations, but it should be doable.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T08:46:07-07:00","id":953868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>idpersona</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953864#post953864\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see why they couldn't mate and produce kin, and then mate with the kin (incest-y I know, but it <i>does</i> happen in nature) to produce new Grondr eventually. It would take a couple of generations, but it should be doable.</div> </div> </div> </div>See while I agree with you, I guess its the way CB20 decided to go with things. Granted I was a little annoied over the idea that 0 of their kin were left in the wild initially (I'm tempted to ignore it for my own games, this isn't the same as the extinct Auroch after all). I get the rules where if there isn't fresh shifter blood injected into the kin lines every few generation or so, it dies out...I was just never a huge fan of that rule.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T10:52:20-07:00","id":953911,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>idpersona</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953832#post953832\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So follow up thoughts.<br /> If only a portion of the animals are kinfolk, that would go a long way towards explaining the low Gurahl numbers, since most bears nowadays wouldn't be kinfolk (which is a sad thought imo). That also means that Rokea mating once or twice a century puts their number of kinfolk extremely low, even with being able to mate with any shark.<br /> <br /> Because this came up in another thread. What percentage of the rat population do you think are Ratkin kinfolk? Using NYC as an example again. There are over 2 million rats in the city. I'm curious how many of them are kinfolk then.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rokea don't actually have proper shark kin, because there's a 100% chance that at least one Rokea results from a Rokea and a shark of the appropriate species, any shark of the appropriate species. With humans, it's substantially more complicated, and requires said human to be kinfolk to have a chance to get a Homid Rokea, who will be chased around by fanatical anti-Betweeners from birth to death unless in Beast Courts territory.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T13:21:21-07:00","id":953989,"title":"","content":"The Gurahl though could repopulate the kinfolk pretty quickly (at least the River Keepers could). There are five hundred thousand black bears in North America and, probably, forty male River Keepers in North America (I would imagine that they are the most common tribe). Black bears have a 60 day breeding season (early May to early July), so each male River Keeper could make with one female black bear a day during the breeding season, meaning twenty-four hundred matings in a year. The average success rate of black bear breeding is fifty percent, so they would produce an average of twenty-four hundred cubs (an average of two cubs per breeding). <br /> <br /> If they did that every year for two decades (from 1996, when the majority of the Sleepers were awake, to 2016, present day), they would have produced forty-eight thousand cubs. Fifty percent of the cubs survive to adulthood, which would mean that twenty-four thousand cubs survive to reach the time of the first change. Twenty-four hundred of them would become Gurahl (10% of the cubs) and between 50% to 100% of the rest would be kinfolk, depending on whether you use W20 rules or W Revised rules. Whether or not the Gurahl would be willing to mate like rabbits would be up to them, but it would be possible for them to much more rapidly expand their population than the Garou (there are only 200,000 wolves globally). It is one of the reasons why my Werewolf 2015 scenario has a lot more Gurahl than the Gurahl from Revised (which was published in 1998).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T13:58:52-07:00","id":954025,"title":"","content":"While the math doesn't look wrong.... actually pulling that off would be highly problematic. One male would be very unlikely to be able to mate with a new female every day during the mating season. All those black bears are distributed along a huge range, and are naturally solitary creatures outside of specific events.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T13:59:50-07:00","id":954028,"title":"","content":"Kin and Kin can breed true or did that get dropped somewhere?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T14:19:13-07:00","id":954037,"title":"","content":"I also want to toss in here, I don't think the developers themselves were running a lot of math when making the game. That's something else to keep in mind....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T15:35:27-07:00","id":954065,"title":"","content":"You are probably right about the developers did not do the numbers. I think that Kin-Kin is still 1% (plus 1% per cumulative level of Pure Breed Background) unless they changed that in the 20th anniversary. Of course, that does not matter for Camazotz, Corax, or Ratkin.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T16:14:34-07:00","id":954086,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=953989#post953989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> ...Gurahl breeding numbers...</div> </div> </div> </div>While all of those numbers look fine, I don't see the Gurahl forsaking their job to go on a 2 decade sex rampage. Especially with the Apocalypse coming up fast. That is, in theory, what caused their awakening.<br /> <br /> Okay, so Rokea breed 100% which makes the question of them and kinfolk moot. What about Nagah and cross breeding?<br /> <br /> And anyone have an idea on the percentage of rats in a city that are kinfolk?<br /> <br /> Also how many generations will kinfolk breed true? Doesn't pure breed affect that? If so, what about the Fera without pure breed?<br /> <br /> Oh, and thanks for all of the input so far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":805,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","user":"idpersona","date":". 08-19-2016, 07:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T16:56:59-07:00","id":954102,"title":"","content":"Kin-kin pairings for most of the Changing Breeds can result in full Shifters. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11683\" data-vbnamecard=\"11683\">Aya Tari</a> pointed out, there are a few of them that have special methods of how all this works.<br /> <br /> I don't think W20 has reprinted the various math/numbers from the previous editions. The numbers never really work right anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T17:04:30-07:00","id":954109,"title":"","content":"Well, I generally operate on the assumption that there are 100 Human Kinfolk per Human Born Changing Breed and 10 Animal Kinfolk per Animal Born Changing Breed (animal reproduction is more easy to control than human reproduction, at least when you are talking about large mammals). In the case of Camazotz, Corax, and Ratkin, I would put the Human Kinfolk number at around ten times the normal ratio and the Animal Kinfolk number at around one hundred times because of the expense of reproduction (in the case of the Camazotz and Corax) or the danger of 'upgrading' (in the case of the Ratkin) and because of the difficulty of controlling small bird or small mammal reproduction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-21T07:02:52-07:00","id":954930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=954065#post954065\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You are probably right about the developers did not do the numbers. I think that Kin-Kin is still 1% (plus 1% per cumulative level of Pure Breed Background) unless they changed that in the 20th anniversary. Of course, that does not matter for Camazotz, Corax, or Ratkin.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> What about Breeds that don't have pure breed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-21T09:39:21-07:00","id":954987,"title":"","content":"A base 1%? Though I think that the only Changing Breed without special reproduction rules that lacks Pure Breed is the Kitsune. On a related note, when we look at Kinfolk: Unsung Heroes, the write up of Pure Breed Background seems to suggest that Kinfolk with Pure Breed had a Garou parent within (6 - Pure Breed Background) generations (Bone Gnawers and Glass Walkers being an exception).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-21T13:21:26-07:00","id":955126,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/953636-animal-kinfolk-question?p=954987#post954987\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A base 1%? Though I think that the only Changing Breed without special reproduction rules that lacks Pure Breed is the Kitsune. On a related note, when we look at Kinfolk: Unsung Heroes, the write up of Pure Breed Background seems to suggest that Kinfolk with Pure Breed had a Garou parent within (6 - Pure Breed Background) generations (Bone Gnawers and Glass Walkers being an exception).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I am thinking Kitsune. I was having an argument with someone about how common they are. I'm of the opinion the only reason they aren't the most common changing breed is the curse and they've only been breeding up for just over 3000 years..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-21T15:42:36-07:00","id":955248,"title":"","content":"Yes, I agree. How many Kitsune are there though? I do not think that it is really specified.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}